WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.858
Here is a function of two variables

2
00:00:05.858 --> 00:00:08.488
a cubic in x, plus a quadratic.

3
00:00:08.488 --> 00:00:11.446
It is not a loss from a real model

4
00:00:11.446 --> 00:00:13.500
but it behaves like one.

5
00:00:13.850 --> 00:00:17.468
Its gradient is zero at two points: two

6
00:00:17.468 --> 00:00:20.272
minus two, and minus two, two.

7
00:00:20.272 --> 00:00:23.890
At both, the surface is perfectly flat.

8
00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:27.256
Which one is the bottom of a valley?

9
00:00:27.256 --> 00:00:28.680
Pause and guess.

10
00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:33.621
In one variable

11
00:00:33.621 --> 00:00:36.259
the second derivative decides.

12
00:00:36.259 --> 00:00:39.322
In two, the second derivatives fill

13
00:00:39.407 --> 00:00:42.300
a symmetric matrix, the Hessian H.

14
00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:44.174
Near a flat point

15
00:00:44.258 --> 00:00:46.544
Taylor's formula keeps only

16
00:00:46.628 --> 00:00:48.236
the quadratic term

17
00:00:48.236 --> 00:00:50.437
one half h transpose H h.

18
00:00:50.437 --> 00:00:53.823
That is the energy of the last lecture

19
00:00:53.823 --> 00:00:56.870
so its level curves tell the story

20
00:00:56.870 --> 00:00:59.155
ellipses around a minimum

21
00:00:59.155 --> 00:01:01.610
hyperbolas through a saddle.

22
00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:06.335
At two, minus two, the Hessian is six, two

23
00:01:06.335 --> 00:01:07.299
two, two.

24
00:01:07.299 --> 00:01:10.769
Its pivots are six and four thirds

25
00:01:10.769 --> 00:01:12.215
both positive.

26
00:01:12.215 --> 00:01:15.492
So the level curves are ellipses

27
00:01:15.492 --> 00:01:19.830
and f goes up in every direction: a minimum.

28
00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:23.694
Check with a small step from there.

29
00:01:23.794 --> 00:01:26.404
f rises by zero point zero

30
00:01:26.505 --> 00:01:28.613
four two eight three

31
00:01:28.713 --> 00:01:32.428
and one half h transpose H h predicts

32
00:01:32.528 --> 00:01:35.540
zero point zero four two five.

33
00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:38.431
At the point where x is minus

34
00:01:38.511 --> 00:01:39.866
two and y is two

35
00:01:39.866 --> 00:01:43.452
the Hessian has entries minus two, two, two

36
00:01:43.452 --> 00:01:46.242
two, with determinant minus eight.

37
00:01:46.242 --> 00:01:49.191
The eigenvalues have opposite signs

38
00:01:49.191 --> 00:01:52.060
so the level curves are hyperbolas

39
00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:54.451
f goes up along one direction

40
00:01:54.531 --> 00:01:56.523
and down along the other.

41
00:01:56.523 --> 00:01:57.320
A saddle.

42
00:01:57.900 --> 00:01:59.980
Here is a tempting shortcut

43
00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:03.249
if the diagonal of the Hessian is positive

44
00:02:03.249 --> 00:02:04.660
call it a minimum.

45
00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:09.280
Take x squared plus four x y plus y squared.

46
00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:12.770
Its Hessian has twos on the diagonal

47
00:02:12.770 --> 00:02:14.147
both positive.

48
00:02:14.147 --> 00:02:16.259
But at one, minus one

49
00:02:16.259 --> 00:02:20.023
the function is one minus four plus one

50
00:02:20.023 --> 00:02:23.880
minus two, below its value at the origin.

51
00:02:24.230 --> 00:02:27.473
The eigenvalues are six and minus two

52
00:02:27.473 --> 00:02:30.971
the cross term made the origin a saddle.

53
00:02:30.971 --> 00:02:34.470
Test the whole matrix, not its diagonal.

54
00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:38.118
How curved is a bowl in one direction?

55
00:02:38.118 --> 00:02:41.428
Divide the energy by the squared length.

56
00:02:41.509 --> 00:02:44.900
This Rayleigh quotient always lies between

57
00:02:44.981 --> 00:02:48.210
the smallest and the largest eigenvalue.

58
00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:51.290
For the café matrix: along one

59
00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:52.787
zero it is five.

60
00:02:52.787 --> 00:02:56.222
Along two, one it is six, the maximum.

61
00:02:56.222 --> 00:03:00.185
Along one, minus two it is one, the minimum.

62
00:03:00.185 --> 00:03:03.620
Drawn at half size in every direction

63
00:03:03.708 --> 00:03:06.438
the curve stays inside the ring

64
00:03:06.526 --> 00:03:08.640
between the two circles.

65
00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:11.166
Now roll down a bowl.

66
00:03:11.166 --> 00:03:14.873
Take the quadratic loss with Hessian C

67
00:03:14.873 --> 00:03:18.765
and measure every point from its minimum.

68
00:03:18.858 --> 00:03:21.545
Each gradient step multiplies

69
00:03:21.638 --> 00:03:24.140
the error by I minus eta C.

70
00:03:24.490 --> 00:03:27.277
Along the steep axis that factor

71
00:03:27.364 --> 00:03:29.193
is one minus six eta

72
00:03:29.193 --> 00:03:32.589
along the shallow axis, one minus eta.

73
00:03:32.589 --> 00:03:35.985
Both must be smaller than one in size

74
00:03:35.985 --> 00:03:39.381
so the steep direction sets the limit

75
00:03:39.381 --> 00:03:41.210
eta below one third.

76
00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:44.456
With eta equal to zero point one

77
00:03:44.456 --> 00:03:47.002
the steps are safe but slow.

78
00:03:47.002 --> 00:03:49.986
With two sevenths, the best step

79
00:03:50.074 --> 00:03:52.268
the error shrinks by five

80
00:03:52.356 --> 00:03:54.023
sevenths each time.

81
00:03:54.023 --> 00:03:56.481
With zero point three four

82
00:03:56.481 --> 00:03:58.412
just above one third

83
00:03:58.499 --> 00:04:00.869
the path zigzags across the

84
00:04:00.957 --> 00:04:02.800
steep axis and grows.

85
00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:07.092
The ratio of the extreme eigenvalues, kappa

86
00:04:07.092 --> 00:04:08.257
is six here.

87
00:04:08.257 --> 00:04:11.304
A large kappa means slow descent

88
00:04:11.393 --> 00:04:14.260
which is one reason features are

89
00:04:14.350 --> 00:04:16.590
rescaled before training.

90
00:04:17.170 --> 00:04:18.079
To sum up.

91
00:04:18.170 --> 00:04:21.259
A flat point is a minimum when the

92
00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:23.984
Hessian is positive definite

93
00:04:23.984 --> 00:04:25.892
ellipses all around.

94
00:04:25.892 --> 00:04:29.890
Mixed signs give a saddle, with hyperbolas.

95
00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:32.206
The Rayleigh quotient is the

96
00:04:32.276 --> 00:04:34.031
curvature in a direction

97
00:04:34.031 --> 00:04:36.347
between the extreme eigenvalues

98
00:04:36.418 --> 00:04:38.734
and the steepest direction limits

99
00:04:38.804 --> 00:04:40.840
the step of gradient descent.

100
00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:42.402
In part two

101
00:04:42.402 --> 00:04:45.346
the same quadratic forms as shapes

102
00:04:45.346 --> 00:04:48.700
and what survives a change of variables.
